    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Monastery Swiftspear before combat is suboptimal sequencing. With haste, Swiftspear can attack this turn regardless. By casting it pre-combat, Kimi25 loses the opportunity to cast noncreature spells (like Lightning Bolt) before declaring attacks to trigger prowess on the Swiftspear during combat. More importantly, casting creatures before combat when holding combat tricks (Lightning Bolt) reveals information unnecessarily and doesn't leverage the haste advantage properly. The optimal line would be to cast Swiftspear, then cast additional noncreature spells to pump it via prowess before attacking. However, the bigger sequencing concern is that casting the creature first telegraphs the attack plan to the opponent, and if they had interaction, it gives them more information. That said, casting Swiftspear first does allow subsequent noncreature spells to trigger prowess, so there's some logic to it. The real issue is whether Dragon's Rage Channeler should have been cast first instead - DRC would benefit from surveil triggers from noncreature spells and with 4 card types already in graveyard (creature, sorcery, land, land - actually Slickshot Show-Off is creature, Preordain is sorcery, Bloodstained Mire and Wooded Foothills are lands - that's only 3 types: creature, sorcery, land), DRC needs one more type. Mishra's Bauble could be sacrificed to put an artifact in the graveyard for delirium, making DRC a 3/3 flyer. But Swiftspear with haste is reasonable for immediate pressure.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Monastery Swiftspear as the first spell in precombat main phase",
      "better_line": "Cast Dragon's Rage Channeler first (to set up surveil and potential delirium with Mishra's Bauble sacrifice adding artifact to graveyard for a 3/3 flyer), or alternatively cast Swiftspear but follow up with noncreature spells before combat to maximize prowess triggers",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 49,
      "player": "Kimi25 Truth",
      "decision_index": 68,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Kimi25 Truth has a Monastery Swiftspear on board and 4 lands (one tapped Mountain, Thundering Falls, two Steam Vents). They have Lightning Bolt, Dragon's Rage Channeler, Slickshot Show-Off, and Cori-Steel Cutter in hand. The opponent has Gran-Gran, Emperor of Bones, and Psychic Frog - all dangerous creatures that will generate incremental value. Passing without casting anything and skipping the attack phase wastes an entire turn of development. At minimum, Dragon's Rage Channeler should be deployed (it costs R, and there are untapped lands available) to start surveilling toward delirium. Lightning Bolt could remove a key threat like Psychic Frog or Emperor of Bones. Slickshot Show-Off could be plotted for a future explosive turn. Instead, the player did literally nothing - no spells, no attacks with Monastery Swiftspear - giving the opponent a free turn to develop their board and generate value with Emperor of Bones and Psychic Frog.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without casting any spells and skipped the attack phase entirely, wasting a full turn.",
      "better_line": "Cast Dragon's Rage Channeler (R) to develop the board and enable surveil. Consider using Lightning Bolt on Psychic Frog or Emperor of Bones to prevent value generation. At minimum, attack with Monastery Swiftspear - casting noncreature spells first would trigger prowess for extra damage. Plot Slickshot Show-Off for a future alpha strike. Doing nothing with 3 untapped lands and relevant spells in hand against an opponent building a threatening board is a significant waste of tempo.",
