      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Failed to play Scalding Tarn from hand when they had a land drop available. Playing a land is free and provides additional mana for future turns. Moreover, Scalding Tarn can be cracked to add a card to the graveyard, helping toward the 6-card graveyard requirement for the two Abhorrent Oculus in hand.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without playing Scalding Tarn, missing their land drop for the turn.",
      "better_line": "Play Scalding Tarn to make their land drop, preserving access to additional mana and a future graveyard card for Abhorrent Oculus.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 117,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 154,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Kimi25 Truth is at 5 life facing a tapped-out opponent at 11 life, with Lightning Bolt (3 damage), Lava Dart (1 damage, plus flashback for another 1 = 2 total), Unholy Heat (2 damage to face if targeting player... wait, Unholy Heat only hits creatures/planeswalkers). Let me recalculate: Lightning Bolt (3 to face) + Lava Dart (1 to face) + Lava Dart flashback sacrificing Mountain (1 to face) = 5 damage from burn. Plus two Monastery Swiftspears and Dragon's Rage Channeler can be deployed. With the opponent completely tapped out, this is the turn to deploy threats and start racing. The opponent has Psychic Frog, Emperor of Bones, and Gran-Gran that will attack next turn for lethal if uncontested. Passing the turn with a hand full of spells while at 5 life against an opponent who can attack for lethal is catastrophic. At minimum, deploying creatures as blockers is essential for survival, and burning the opponent brings them into kill range.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without casting any spells, then skipped attack, effectively doing nothing on a critical turn.",
      "better_line": "Deploy Dragon's Rage Channeler and both Monastery Swiftspears as blockers/attackers. Cast noncreature spells to trigger prowess. Lightning Bolt the opponent's face (opponent to 8). Use Lava Dart to face (opponent to 7), flashback Lava Dart sacrificing Mountain (opponent to 6). The prowess triggers on Swiftspears from these noncreature spells would make them formidable attackers/blockers. This develops a board presence for blocking the opponent's lethal attack while putting significant pressure on their life total.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 119,
      "player": "Kimi25 Truth",
      "decision_index": 156,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Discarded Preordain over the second copy of Cori-Steel Cutter when one Cori-Steel Cutter is already on the battlefield. Preordain is a 1-mana cantrip that provides card selection to find the right cards to close the game, while a second Cori-Steel Cutter is redundant with the first copy already in play.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Preordain",
      "better_line": "Discard the second Cori-Steel Cutter since one is already on the battlefield, keeping Preordain for efficient card selection to find the right sequencing to close out the game from 5 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 121,
      "player": "Kimi25 Truth",
      "decision_index": 156,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Declined Emperor of Bones' beginning of combat exile trigger, which is a free 'up to one' ability with no downside. Could have exiled Monastery Swiftspear from opponent's graveyard to set up potential adapt value. With the opponent at 5 life and no blockers, the adapt line (making Emperor 4/4 and getting DRC with haste) would provide massive overkill damage. Even without adapt, exiling a creature is pure upside.",
      "action_taken": "Passed without using Emperor of Bones' combat exile trigger",
      "better_line": "Use Emperor of Bones' combat trigger to exile Monastery Swiftspear from opponent's graveyard (free value), then consider adapting Emperor of Bones for additional damage and DRC with haste",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 123,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 164,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "When manifesting dread (from Abhorrent Oculus's trigger), you look at the top two cards and put one face-down as a 2/2 creature and the other into your graveyard. Putting Abhorrent Oculus into the graveyard wastes a powerful card - it's a 5/5 flyer that could be manifested face-down and then turned face up for its mana cost (though it requires exiling 6 cards from graveyard as additional cost, which complicates flipping). However, Scalding Tarn as the manifest can never be turned face up (it's not a creature card), so it's just a 2/2 forever. Abhorrent Oculus as the manifest at least has the theoretical upside of being flipped into a 5/5 flyer if conditions allow. Additionally, Scalding Tarn in the graveyard would have been more useful - it's a land that does nothing as a 2/2, while putting it in the graveyard adds a land card type to the graveyard (though land is already potentially covered). The key issue is that manifesting the Oculus gives a body that could potentially become a 5/5 flyer, while manifesting the Tarn gives a permanently vanilla 2/2.",
      "action_taken": "Chose to manifest Scalding Tarn face-down (permanently a 2/2) and put Abhorrent Oculus into the graveyard.",
      "better_line": "Manifest Abhorrent Oculus face-down (can potentially be turned face up as a 5/5 flyer) and put Scalding Tarn into the graveyard.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded Psychic Frog instead of a redundant Abhorrent Oculus. Psychic Frog is a strong 2-mana value creature that can be replayed. With only 5 cards in graveyard, discarding either card brings it to 6, enabling one Abhorrent Oculus cast. Discarding a redundant Oculus (can't cast both anyway due to graveyard exile requirement) preserves the more flexible Psychic Frog for future use.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Psychic Frog to pump the attacking Psychic Frog",
      "better_line": "Discard an Abhorrent Oculus instead, preserving Psychic Frog as a replayable threat while still enabling the remaining Oculus cast from the 6-card graveyard",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lightning Bolt in the graveyard has no way to be cast without specific recursion, while Lava Dart has flashback (sacrifice a Mountain) and the opponent controls a Mountain. Exiling Lava Dart would remove an actual live threat from the graveyard.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Lightning Bolt from graveyard with Emperor of Bones trigger",
      "better_line": "Exile Lava Dart instead, since it has flashback and can be cast from the graveyard by sacrificing the opponent's Mountain for 1 damage plus potential prowess triggers.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "By manifesting Archon of Cruelty (which costs 8 mana to flip face-up, very unlikely), they put Persist into the graveyard where it's a useless sorcery. They should have manifested Persist (a sorcery that can NEVER be flipped face-up anyway, so it's always just a 2/2) and put Archon of Cruelty into the graveyard, where it could be reanimated by a future Persist draw for a game-winning ETB trigger against an opponent at 5 life.",
      "action_taken": "Manifested Archon of Cruelty face-down, sending Persist to graveyard",
      "better_line": "Manifest Persist face-down (permanent 2/2 either way since sorceries can't flip) and put Archon of Cruelty in graveyard as a reanimation target",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Attempted to cast Abhorrent Oculus with only 5 cards in graveyard (needs 6 to exile as additional cost), and with insufficient mana (only Blood Crypt untapped, providing B/R, while the spell costs {2}{U}). This is an illegal play that the game engine apparently allowed.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Abhorrent Oculus during combat with insufficient graveyard cards and mana",
      "better_line": "Pass through combat and hold up Fatal Push with Blood Crypt for the opponent's turn, maintaining defensive options against the opponent at 5 life",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Emperor of Bones' combat trigger exiles a card that can potentially be put onto the battlefield attacking when Emperor attacks. Exiling Preordain (a sorcery) wastes this opportunity. Monastery Swiftspear was available as a creature to exile, which could be reanimated attacking for extra damage against an opponent at 5 life.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Preordain (a sorcery) with Emperor of Bones' trigger",
      "better_line": "Exile Monastery Swiftspear to potentially put it onto the battlefield tapped and attacking via Emperor of Bones' attack trigger, adding extra damage against the opponent at 5 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Manifested Bloodstained Mire (a land that can never be turned face up) instead of Psychic Frog (a creature that could be flipped for just {U}{B}). The manifest of a land is permanently stuck as a vanilla 2/2, while manifesting the Frog preserves the option to flip it into a real threat.",
      "action_taken": "Manifested Bloodstained Mire face-down and put Psychic Frog into the graveyard.",
      "better_line": "Manifest Psychic Frog face-down (can be flipped for {U}{B} into a 1/2 with all abilities) and put Bloodstained Mire into the graveyard where it has minimal utility.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "The player declined or mishandled the manifest dread trigger from Abhorrent Oculus, which is a pure-upside ability. The correct choice was to manifest Psychic Frog face-down (which can be flipped face-up for just {U}{B} since it's a creature card) and put Bitter Triumph into the graveyard. The reasoning 'I don't control this action' is incorrect - this is MiniMx Gramps' own Abhorrent Oculus trigger and they absolutely control this choice.",
      "action_taken": "Chose False / declined the manifest dread selection",
      "better_line": "Manifest Psychic Frog face-down (can be turned face up for {U}{B} to get a 1/2 with upside) and put Bitter Triumph into the graveyard.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Fetching basic Island instead of Undercity Sewers misses the surveil 1 trigger that could put a 6th card into the graveyard, which is needed to enable casting Abhorrent Oculus (requires exiling 6 cards from graveyard, currently only 5 there). Since it's already the combat phase with most lands tapped, the 'enters tapped' downside of Undercity Sewers is negligible - both lands would be untapped next turn.",
      "action_taken": "Fetched basic Island",
      "better_line": "Fetch Undercity Sewers to get surveil 1, potentially putting a 6th card in graveyard to enable Abhorrent Oculus next turn. The dual-color flexibility (U/B) is also better than just U.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Opponent is at 5 life. MiniMx Gramps has delirium active (land, instant, sorcery, creature in graveyard - 4+ types). Unholy Heat with delirium deals 6 damage to a creature or planeswalker, not directly to face - but it's still a powerful removal spell. More importantly, Lava Dart can deal 1 damage to face and be flashed back for another 1, helping close the game. Dragon's Rage Channeler is a strong threat. Preordain is just card selection when the game should be about closing out quickly at this life total differential.",
      "action_taken": "Chose Preordain - a cantrip for card selection",
      "better_line": "Should have taken Dragon's Rage Channeler as an additional threat to pressure the opponent at 5 life, or Lava Dart for flexible burn damage (1 + 1 flashback to face). With the opponent at 5 life, adding board pressure or direct damage is more valuable than card selection.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "This is Emperor of Bones' beginning of combat trigger, which exiles a card from a graveyard to fuel its ability to cast creatures from exile. The chosen card 'Faithless Looting' is not listed among the available choices shown (which include cards like Lava Dart, Monastery Swiftspear, Thoughtseize, Preordain, etc.). The reasoning also incorrectly references 'Expressive Iteration' which isn't involved. More importantly, the optimal exile target should be a creature card like Monastery Swiftspear (from opponent's graveyard) since Emperor of Bones can cast exiled creature cards, making a creature far more valuable to exile than a sorcery like Faithless Looting.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Faithless Looting with Emperor of Bones' trigger, apparently confused about the source of the ability.",
      "better_line": "Exile Monastery Swiftspear from opponent's graveyard, as Emperor of Bones can cast exiled creature cards, giving you access to a hasty attacker. Alternatively, exile a relevant creature to deny graveyard synergies.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Casting Abhorrent Oculus during combat with seemingly insufficient graveyard cards (only 5 visible, need 6) and only 1 untapped land (Blood Crypt) when the card costs {2}{U}. The reasoning also incorrectly describes the card's ability as generating treasures when it actually manifest dreads. With opponent at 5 life, focusing on closing the game with existing attackers and holding Fatal Push for defense may be more reliable.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Abhorrent Oculus during combat phase",
      "better_line": "Pass through combat, hold up Fatal Push with Blood Crypt mana for defensive purposes, and look to close the game with existing board threats next turn",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded Polluted Delta instead of the basic Swamp. Polluted Delta is strictly better to keep because it enables revolt for Fatal Push (in hand), can fetch either Island or Swamp for color flexibility, and thins the deck. The basic Swamp provides none of these advantages.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Polluted Delta to pump Psychic Frog, keeping Thoughtseize and Swamp.",
      "better_line": "Discard the basic Swamp instead, preserving Polluted Delta for its revolt synergy with Fatal Push and superior utility.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "MiniMx Gramps",
      "decision_index": 166,
    }
  ],
